#
# ## `budget.codegen.functions` re-pricing
#
# `budget.codegen.functions` is `fz.codegen.define_function.stop` from
# `fz2 --emit=stats run <this fixture>`. It reads 37.
#
# The argument of the final `dbg` is a 3-tuple whose every position is
# `:unknown | any`, so the lattice carries it as EIGHT clauses and the callsite
# settles eight `Kernel.dbg/1` arms (and eight `Kernel.fz_dbg_value/1` arms
# behind them). fz-kdt.119 gave the tuple test a sub-predicate per position, so
# those eight stopped being one question and each became a destination: the
# number went 29 -> 35 -> 49, the last move being those arms becoming live.
# Minting a specialization per clause for a callee that reads no position is
# waste, and fz-kdt.140 owns it -- the 2^arity product is still minted.
#
# What fz-kdt.143 removes is the DISPATCH over them, and with it the demand for
# the bodies. `{any, any, any}` is the same callee on a surface that strictly
# contains each sibling's, asking a question that admits everything theirs
# admits, so it stands in for all seven; and for the six MIXED clauses the seat
# would never put the sibling first, because a sub-position holding `any`
# against `any` is an erasing overlap and coverage then demands whole-tuple
# containment, which fails. Those six are dropped. `{:unknown, :unknown,
# :unknown}` survives -- it overlaps erasingly nowhere, so coverage runs both
# ways and precision seats it first -- and the callsite tests TWO arms instead
# of eight. Twelve executables lose their only caller (six `Kernel.dbg/1`, six
# `Kernel.fz_dbg_value/1`): 49 -> 37 codegen functions, 25 -> 13 executables in
# the backend program.
#
# The other nine `budget.*` keys on this fixture have no producer anywhere in
# compiler2 -- they name a "planner" that does not exist -- and fz-kdt.139 owns
# removing them corpus-wide. Nothing enforces any of the ten; this one is
# reproducible by the recipe above, and that is the whole of its standing.
#
# # receive_list_cons_pattern
#
# fz-puj.44 (X3) — receive matcher implementing SwitchKind::ListCons.
#
# Three messages sent (a non-empty list, an empty list, an atom) and three
# receives drain them in source order. Each clause set mixes `[h | _]`, `[]`,
# and a wildcard default so the matrix specialises ListCons with three keys
# (Cons → projects head via SubjectRef::ListHead; EmptyList; Nil-as-default).
#
# Locks that interp/JIT/AOT all bind `h` to the list's first element, route
# `[]` to the EmptyList arm, and fall through atoms to the wildcard.
fn main() do
  send(self(), [10, 20, 30])
  send(self(), [])
  send(self(), :stop)

  a = receive do
    [h | _] -> h
    [] -> -1
    _ -> 0
  end

  b = receive do
    [h | _] -> h
    [] -> -1
    _ -> 0
  end

  c = receive do
    [h | _] -> h
    [] -> -1
    _ -> 0
  end

  dbg({a, b, c})
end
